Hundreds of characters have died during my seemingly hopeless attempts
to retrieve the Amulet of Yendor, until today ...

Olphuk, the lawful female dwarven Valkyrie, ascended to
Demigoddesshood with 5,359,730 points.

As it turned out, the key to victory was a) an easy class/race
combination, and b) slightly bad luck throughout the first part of the
game, so that I never grew overconfident. :) Also, the WikiHack pages
were immensely helpful.

Here's the story:

(Prelude: After letting it rest for about a year, I recently revived
my Psion 5mx pro palmtop. Whenever I do that, Duncon Booth's excellent
port of NetHack 3.4.3 is among the first things I install. It's a
great way to whack awy the hours while siiting in trains, or waiting
in a line in the supermarket, or sitting in various waiting rooms. I
play NetHack since I first encountered it in 1989, but never managed
to ascend, until now.)

As Olphuk, the dwarven Valkyrie, I had a rather unspectacular start.
Having good fighting abilities, I went straight into the Gnomish
Mines. Mine Town had a general shop, a hardware store, and the
lighting store. There was no magic lamp, no magic whistle (not even a
leash), but I found a bag of holding on sale, which I bought.

Without magic protection, I didn't want to venture deeper, so I went
back into the main dungeon - leaving my cat behind for the moment
because I didn't have the means to control it effectively.

Sokoban didn't pose a problem, but while I had hoped for an amulet of
reflection on the last level, I only got a second bag of holding.

In the meantime, I had collected enough gold to buy me some protection
from the Mine Town priest. The altar was co-aligned, but I couldn't
find any water, and didn't want to dilute unidentified potions (or
quaff them to identify them, I hadn't found a unicorn yet). On a whim,
I also ate a nymph corpse because I thought it wouldn't be too
difficult to get teleport control sooner or later. I had already met a
tengu, which my cat killed, but unfortunately it didn't leave a
corpse.

Lacking many things I'd like to have at this point (mainly magic
protection and reflection, but also a unicorn horn, telepathy,
effective pet control, and any resistances apart from sleep and,
obviously, cold), I went down into the deeper mine levels. I ran into
two polymorph traps, but managed to simply let my polymorphed self get
killed, I was strong enough to survive one fight without armor.

The mine bottom contained several mimics, the obligatory luckstone,
but not much else to speak of. I still hadn't seen a single unicorn,
but found another bag of holding.

I went back to the main dungeon, this time taking my cat along, who
promptly died in a battle from which I couldn't recall it. On the way
up, I used a wand of create monster to sacrifice some corpses in
minetown until my god gifted me Mjollnir.

I went down to about level 16 and still had no magic protection, no
reflection, and no unihorn, but lots of gold and food because the game
kept generating treasure zoos, throne rooms, and barracks. Two trips
back to minetown gave me some more protection and finally some holy
water. I also lacked a wand of polymorph (not too surprising at this
stage, but it would certainly have helped, I'm a packrat and haul
basically everything to my huge stashes) and teleport control (duh).
On the bright side, I had found some speed boots in the meantime.

Back in the main dungeon, I ran into my third polymorph trap and
decided that I really needed magic protection. With no magic lamp, no
identified potions (so I didn't know what was inside my 2 smoky
potions), and no wand of polymorph, I saw my best chance in trying the
various thrones that I had found. Lo and behold, the fourth throne I
tried granted me a wish, and I finally had my GDSM. Soon after I
acquired a unihorn and identified some potions. I also found a bones
file with another bag of holding.

I also found Fort Ludios (by randomly teleporting into the vault with
the portal), but didn't want to go there without reflection. Since
Sokoban didn't give me reflection, the next best opportunity was the
Perseus statue. I descended to the Medusa level, only to find out that
there ws something else I lacked; levitation. No boots (not even for
water walking or jumping), no ring, no potions, no wand of cold (the
only one I had found was spent), no spellbook of cone of cone of cold.
No means of magical breathing either, and still no wand of polymorph.
I read the two scrolls of earth that I kept from Sokoban, and tried to
build a bridge with the boulders, but only got to the middle island,
then I ran out of boulders (and scrolls).

Unsure what to do next, I finally decided to have a look at the quest,
especially because I had met a red dragon which finally gave me some
fire resistance (after half a dozen attempts of acquiring it through
other corpses failed). Lord Surtur fell surprisingly quick, and the
warning conveyed by the Orb of Fate came in handy throughout the rest
of the game, especially because I was never able to see invisible.

However, during the quest I made my first real blunder in this game,
by accidentally stepping on a lava tile. My speed boots spontaneously
combusted, and I had to pray to get out of the lava.

Back at my stash, I looked for a wand of digging because I decided to
dig my way through the medusa level and then deal with her when I
found a way back up - a method that I was reluctant to use, because it
would separate me from my stash until I found some means to cross the
water. Going through the stuff I had piled up, I found a bag that I
didn't remember, looked into it, and found (among some other things) a
ring of levitation, which was as relieving as it was embarassing.

So I fought Medusa and, full of hope, smashed the Perseus statue,
which yielded me a sack.

Nothing else.

Probably the bags of holding with which the game had decided to shower
me had gone out of stock.

Since I still lacked reflection, gauntlets of power, teleport control,
and genocide, I set my eyes on the castle and its wand of wishing. My
ascension kit was still woefully incomplete, although I did at least
find a wand of death and a cloak of displacement (which was good as
long as I wore a mithril coat for its level 3 magic cancellation, but
a while after I got my GDSM I changed to an elven cloak instead).

On the castle level, I was greeted by an Arch-Lich, who, after some
friendly replies from Mjollnir, escaped upstairs. Not having
encountered any other Lich before in that game, I was a bit shocked,
but luckily has touch of death didn't work, and I didn't intend to
follow him. Instead, I fought my way into the castle. There I made my
second blunder, I decided to let a cockatrice corpse lie around
because I was already heavily loaded. Promptly some giant came along
and picked it up. I zapped him with a wand of magic missile, but he
seemed unimpressed, and two steps away from me, he wielded the corpse.
Quickly, I pulled my wand of death out of my backpack (while the giant
stepped on the tile next to me), and zapped him one turn before he
could attack. I then made *very* sure that *every* cockatrice corpse
got stowed away.

After that near-death experience, I retrieved the wand of wishing and
immediately wished for: charging scrolls, an amulet of reflection,
gauntlets of power, speed boots, a helm of brilliance, genocide
scrolls, and ... a ring of teleport control. I thought about wishing
for a wand of polymorph, but decided against it because I was sure I
would eventually come across one of those.

I also found Demonbane (in the castle storage) ... and my fourth bag
of holding. I started to think about starting a bag store, should I
survive this adventure.

From then on, nothing put me into danger for a long time. The massive
amount of hit points that a dwarven valkyrie gets, together with the
defensive means I now had, including the reduced damage due to the Orb
of Fate, and the power of Mjollnir made sure that I rarely had less
than 150 hit points. All liches and water monsters were genocided as
soon as I got my genocide scrolls, so these couldn't ruin my day
either.

Fort Ludios was surprisingly easy. I used the gold to buy some more
protection and went down into Gehennom (actually finding my first
magic whistle in the Valley of the Dead). The demon princes didn't
really pose any danger, especially because neither Yeenoghu nor
Demogorgon got summoned. I traveled back to my stash several times
(did I already mention that I'm a packrat?) and used any money I found
to buy some more protection from the valley priest (although the
chances of actually getting some were rapidly declining now).

After locating the vibrating square, I went all the way to level 1
(placing a bag with K-Rations, all my valuable gems, and some wands of
digging there), got some candles from Izchak, and created about 30
monsters at the minetown altar. In fighting them I also maxed out my
skills for twoweaponing and sabre fighting. Thanking me for my
offerings, Tyr gave me a spellbook of haste self, which (since I could
never use it with the wy the character developed) I took as his way of
telling me that I was already well-equipped for the final tasks.

Going back to my huge stash, I carefully selected which rings,
scrolls, wands etc. I would take with me. During this process, I
noticed that I had caried a bag of tricks with me all the time,
stashed away in an oilskin sack that I used for the one and only wand
of cancellation I found. Wanting to get rid of, the bag of tricks, I
put it into my stash, and getting rid of it I did, because I had put
my whole stash into one of the gazillions of bags of holding that I
had found.

Boom.

Well, that made the decision of what to take with me a whole lot
easier.

Fortunately, I had already taken most of the things I wanted out of
the stash, so I didn't lose too much - only most of my backup tools
(including a handful of unicorn horns), and all food (but I simply put
a ring of slow digestion on my finger, and I still had my K-rations on
level 1). And, of course, I lost lots of polypiling fodder, but by
this time I had already accepted that I wouldn't find a wand of
polymorph anyway. I had gone down to the vibrating square without
finding one, I had never found a magic lamp, and I had used up all my
wishes (there also weren't any thrones left).

So I continued my preparations, blessed four potions of gain level,
drank them ...

*Poof* A djinni emerges. "I am in your debt. I will grant one
wish!" ...

This happened about two minutes after I just blew up hundreds of items
of potential polypiling fodder.

Having no use for a wand of polymorph any more, I wished for a shield
of reflection instead (in case I ever wanted to put on an amulet of
life saving), but I never actually used it.

I continued my preparations, wrote some scrolls with a magic marker I
had found, enchanted and blessed most of my equipment, and went down.

Rodney didn't pose much of a threat, neither did the high priest of
Moloch. With trembling hands, I picked up the Amulet of Yendor for the
first time ever. I never had a character that managed to go that far.

The way back to level 1 was easier than I thought. The wizard
reappeared only twice, and was quickly dispatched both times (I still
had my first wand of death, and I also had the one from Orcus, and
another one I found in a graveyard, as a backup). It was just a little
annoying to walk through Orcus' town seven times because the amulet
kept sending me back. But the higher I climbed, the less the amulet
interfered.

On level 1, I picked up my stash with the K-rations, wands of digging,
and gems, and entered the elemental planes.

With four scrolls of gold detection, four potions of confusion, and a
blessed unicorn horn for quick recovery, finding the portals was easy.
The enemies on the earth plane were more annoying than than dangerous,
and the wands of digging quickly created a way to the portal. The
plane of air was only slightly more dangerous, the air elementals
brought me to 50% of hit points - which still amounted to 115 hp. In a
way that was still impressive, because none of Rodney, the high
priest, or the demon princes had managed to bring me to less than 170
hit points, but the still didn't hit hard enough to be worried about.

I made a mistake when trying to find the portal, but a djinni was
friendly enough to reveal its location for me.

The fire and water planes went quite smooth.

I then entered the astral plane for the first time. I was granted a
guardian angel, but didn't find it particularly effective. When I was
down to 75 hp, I quaffed a potion of full healing (I had mixed 20 of
those), stepped away from the angel, and put on a ring of conflict.

Death reduced my max hp by 30 points, and I had to kill him thrice,
only to find out that he was guarding the wrong altar anyway. I fought
my way back, teleporting enemies away when they blocked my path (and
were too strong to be killed quickly), and zapped a wand of death at
Pestilence. The second altar I approached was the right one, and I
offered the amulet to Tyr and ascended.

I only hope I don't have to try another 18 years until I achieve my
next victory. :)


